{"title":"2023 AIME II Problems/Problem 6","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_MOP_2024\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by MOP 2024</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_2（Calculus)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2（Calculus)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Solution_3_(Geometry)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3 (Geometry)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Solution_4_(Coordinates,_similar_to_Solution_1)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 4 (Coordinates, similar to Solution 1)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Solution_5\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 5</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_The_Power_of_Logic\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">8</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by The Power of Logic</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-9\"><a href=\"#See_also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">9</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Consider the L-shaped region formed by three unit squares joined at their sides, as shown below. Two points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> are chosen independently and uniformly at random from inside the region. The probability that the midpoint of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/0/840e2b592390eb6ec918fa6f3292716ce170de66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{AB}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"18\" /> also lies inside this L-shaped region can be expressed as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/8/8/888fed89e19182447f85b3cb6676548a49395284.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{m}{n},$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"27\" height=\"34\" /> where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> are relatively prime positive integers. Find <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/b/5db26f75f6218a382840f0ed34f5d36bcff61d33.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m+n.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"56\" height=\"14\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/5/e/35e5685ec38ac2940e2bd21b651c8faa1f022f57.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] unitsize(2cm); draw((0,0)--(2,0)--(2,1)--(1,1)--(1,2)--(0,2)--cycle); draw((0,1)--(1,1)--(1,0),dashed); [/asy]\" width=\"192\" height=\"192\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_MOP_2024\">Video Solution by MOP 2024</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by MOP 2024\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtube.com/watch?v=0UViBt-LYTo&amp;t=0s\">https://youtube.com/watch?v=0UViBt-LYTo&amp;t=0s</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We proceed by calculating the complement. \n</p><p>Note that the only configuration of the 2 points that makes the midpoint outside of the L shape is one point in the top square, and one in the right square. This occurs with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/9/f/39f7b0271cf77ff1c569c767ab5eb211bf138518.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{2}{3} \\cdot \\frac{1}{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"41\" height=\"38\" /> probability. \n</p><p>Let the topmost coordinate have value of: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/9/1/391b9cba5c0059a7ba64f8cc8b5ac0acadd787b0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(x_1,y_1+1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"90\" height=\"20\" />, and rightmost value of: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/0/3/403e8631819d4b63634cc606597dc2b0634bd97b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(x_2+1,y_2)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"90\" height=\"20\" />.\n</p><p>The midpoint of them is thus: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/5/2/852443b21ce71b2789e8c006f5f9612e0dc86c9e.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\left(\\frac{x_1+x_2+1}{2}, \\frac{y_1+y_2+1}{2} \\right)\\]\" width=\"210\" height=\"43\" />\n</p><p>It is clear that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/7/7/a77c76115e1d63ff0edb54c3d417c9a1ae17daa4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x_1, x_2, y_1, y_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"94\" height=\"13\" /> are all between 0 and 1. For the midpoint to be outside the L-shape, both coordinates must be greater than 1, thus: \n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/6/f/66f951dbd630332a8337ddd24e3c192c746ca8fd.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{x_1+x_2+1}{2}&gt;1 \\implies x_1+x_2&gt;1\\]\" width=\"264\" height=\"37\" />\n</p><p>By symmetry this has probability <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/f/9/2f960094315d60883495f9b74148e17487ee9584.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" />. Also by symmetry, the probability the y-coordinate works as well is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/f/9/2f960094315d60883495f9b74148e17487ee9584.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" />. Thus the probability that the midpoint is outside the L-shape is: \n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/e/7/fe79aaad5f02977f9f456d128033fe8a00eecd14.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{2}{3} \\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{1}{18}\\]\" width=\"134\" height=\"37\" />\n</p><p>We want the probability that the point is inside the L-shape however, which is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/2/2/2227445da317d4f12c9085904e9326bc74b7d8ef.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1-\\frac{1}{18}=\\frac{17}{18}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"102\" height=\"38\" />, yielding the answer of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/7/b/f7b1266660778d9d5f1ee9d5ffc2e239b4ef4396.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$17+18=\\boxed{35}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"114\" height=\"25\" />\n~SAHANWIJETUNGA\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_2.EF.BC.88Calculus.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2（Calculus)\">Solution 2（Calculus)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2（Calculus)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We assume each box has side length 1.\nWe index the upper left box, the bottom left box, the bottom right box as II, III, IV, respectively.\nWe index the missing upper right box as I.\nWe put the graph to a coordinate system by putting the intersecting point of four foxes at the origin, the positive direction of the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" />axis at the intersecting line of boxes I and IV, and the positive direction of the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" />-axis at the intersecting line of boxes I and II.\nWe denote by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/1/5d1e4485dc90c450e8c76826516c1b2ccb8fce16.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"21\" height=\"14\" /> the midpoint of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p><p>Therefore,\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/c/a/5ca526dec627c4b76ff9741c1242b02795a1e598.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*} \\Bbb P \\left( M \\in II \\cup III \\cup IV \\right) &amp; = 1 - \\Bbb P \\left( M \\in I \\right) . \\end{align*}\" width=\"322\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>We observe that a necessary for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/b/7/6b724c58b70ed40ddf1bf9f628b243f7fcde4658.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M \\in I$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"53\" height=\"15\" /> is either <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/1/2/212503522088535ab7409b027ab2bac2f08b4c91.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A \\in II$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"57\" height=\"15\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/6/8167167dd5e506f553a4c23fdcd715abc1b5c6e5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B \\in IV$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"63\" height=\"15\" />, or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/d/7/6d7a43af601a5dc95dbd119e6410cf5cc7273637.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A \\in IV$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"62\" height=\"15\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/4/c/64cce74771c6ee6fbf7980bcab52f6871ae8ffb7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B \\in II$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"58\" height=\"15\" />.\nIn addition, by symmetry,\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/7/6/4763cc3d9da1250f380de01259937809fa6ec65a.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[ \\Bbb P \\left( M \\in I | A \\in II, B \\in IV \\right) = \\Bbb P \\left( M \\in I | A \\in IV, B \\in II \\right) . \\]\" width=\"444\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>Thus,\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/5/f/75f98e7da132ebd48a4e6a8ea99de7e1c6787e31.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*} \\Bbb P \\left( M \\in I \\right) &amp; = 2 \\Bbb P \\left( M \\in I | A \\in II, B \\in IV \\right) \\Bbb P \\left( A \\in II, B \\in IV \\right) \\\\ &amp; = 2 \\Bbb P \\left( M \\in I | A \\in II, B \\in IV \\right) \\Bbb P \\left( A \\in II \\right) \\Bbb P \\left( B \\in IV \\right) \\\\ &amp; = 2 \\Bbb P \\left( M \\in I | A \\in II, B \\in IV \\right) \\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ &amp; = \\frac{2}{9} \\Bbb P \\left( M \\in I | A \\in II, B \\in IV \\right) \\\\ &amp; = \\frac{2}{9} \\Bbb P \\left( x_M &gt; 0, y_M &gt; 0 | x_A &lt; 0, y_A &gt; 0, x_B &gt; 0, y_B &lt; 0 \\right) \\\\ &amp; = \\frac{2}{9} \\Bbb P \\left( x_M &gt; 0 | x_A &lt; 0, x_B &gt; 0 \\right) \\cdot \\Bbb P \\left( y_M &gt; 0 | y_A &gt; 0, y_B &lt; 0 \\right) \\\\ &amp; = \\frac{2}{9} \\Bbb P \\left( x_M &gt; 0 | x_A &lt; 0, x_B &gt; 0 \\right)^2 \\\\ &amp; = \\frac{2}{9} \\Bbb P \\left( x_A + x_B &gt; 0 | x_A &lt; 0, x_B &gt; 0 \\right)^2 \\\\ &amp; = \\frac{2}{9} \\left( \\int_{x_A = -1}^0 \\int_{x_B = 0}^1 \\mathbf 1 \\left\\{ x_A + x_B &gt; 0 \\right\\} dx_B dx_A \\right)^2 \\\\ &amp; = \\frac{2}{9} \\left( \\int_{x_A = -1}^0 \\int_{x_B = - x_A}^1 dx_B dx_A \\right)^2 \\\\ &amp; = \\frac{2}{9} \\left( \\int_{x_A = -1}^0 \\left( 1 + x_A \\right) dx_A \\right)^2 \\\\ &amp; = \\frac{2}{9} \\left( \\frac{1}{2}  \\right)^2 \\\\ &amp; = \\frac{1}{18} . \\end{align*}\" width=\"542\" height=\"565\" />\nThe second equality follows from the condition that the positions of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> are independent.\nThe sixth equality follows from the condition that for each point of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" />, the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> coordinate are independent.\n</p><p>Therefore,\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/5/4/d547f5bb8b18ae83a2818c0f17586b62178f5230.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*} \\Bbb P \\left( M \\in II \\cup III \\cup IV \\right) &amp; = 1 - \\frac{1}{18} \\\\ &amp; = \\frac{17}{18} . \\end{align*}\" width=\"257\" height=\"80\" />\n</p><p>Therefore, the answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/4/9/a49303e75b1f82eb15817d976cf0102373dba3c5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$17 + 18 = \\boxed{\\textbf{(035) }}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"146\" height=\"30\" />.\n</p><p>~Steven Chen (Professor Chen Education Palace, www.professorchenedu.com)\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_3_.28Geometry.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3_(Geometry)\">Solution 3 (Geometry)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3 (Geometry)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<figure class=\"mw-halign-right\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:2023_AIME_I_6.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/3/38/2023_AIME_I_6.png/470px-2023_AIME_I_6.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"470\" height=\"418\" class=\"mw-file-element\" srcset=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/3/38/2023_AIME_I_6.png/705px-2023_AIME_I_6.png 1.5x, https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/3/38/2023_AIME_I_6.png 2x\" /></a><figcaption></figcaption></figure>\n<p>The only configuration of two points that makes the midpoint outside of the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/f/4bfa3d4028c17abddf943551c393648a1293d55d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$L-$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"28\" height=\"16\" />shape is one point in the top square, and one in the right square with probability <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/4/1/041a0b37ca609642fb34c7c855b9d67ed498a560.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{2}{9}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" /> (see Solution <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/c/25cf202c836290f875ac9359042e4f90936162cb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1.)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"19\" height=\"18\" /> \n</p><p>We use the coordinate system shown in diagram. Let the arbitrary point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/0/2509f84ab1118345df855f52d9a516952961921e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A(x, -y), x \\in [0,1], y \\in [0,1]$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"225\" height=\"20\" /> be in right square. \nIt is clear that iff point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> lies in yellow rectangle with sides <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/8/5d8a0855e334ad5fb7c97fe70d6846ab384ab0f7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1 – y,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"35\" height=\"17\" /> then the midpoint <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> lies outside of the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/b/0/ab0bb0171685e5101725bf6851aaf364fb719639.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$L.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"19\" height=\"14\" />\n</p><p>Probability of this is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/3/d/53d4f50dfa0adc7301dbd5da2cb8fbf4de728d7c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p = x(1 – y).$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"93\" height=\"20\" />\n</p><p>Consider the points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/8/5/c8575a0094612cfab47f9672406e72aa504030f6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_1(1-y,-x), A_2(1-x,y-1), A_3(y,x-1).$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"352\" height=\"20\" />\n</p><p>Similarly we find <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/6/8/168053829418d07abf61b42bf54db329338255ed.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p_1 = (1-y)(1-x), p_2= (1-x)y,  p_3 = xy.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"342\" height=\"20\" /> \n</p><p>The probability that the midpoint of one of the segments <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/6/79697051b40caf7a4766b3df250f8f91263b4e95.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_1B, A_2B, A_3B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"125\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> is outside of the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/f/4bfa3d4028c17abddf943551c393648a1293d55d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$L-$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"28\" height=\"16\" />shape is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/e/c/aec2c5c5d85ad46a67e0cf14ebd7265b0a4f8234.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac {p_1+p_2+p_3+p}{4} = \\frac {1}{4}.\\]\" width=\"169\" height=\"37\" />\nIf point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> is in the right square, point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> in the top square, the probability that the midpoint of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/0/840e2b592390eb6ec918fa6f3292716ce170de66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{AB}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"18\" /> lies outside <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/f/4bfa3d4028c17abddf943551c393648a1293d55d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$L-$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"28\" height=\"16\" />shape is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/6/0/160fcac13960ce632b4d744df3148570e1a52776.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac {1}{4} \\implies$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"59\" height=\"38\" />\n</p><p>If points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> are chosen independently and uniformly at random in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/f/4bfa3d4028c17abddf943551c393648a1293d55d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$L-$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"28\" height=\"16\" />shape, then the probability that the midpoint of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/0/840e2b592390eb6ec918fa6f3292716ce170de66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{AB}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"18\" /> lies outside <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/f/4bfa3d4028c17abddf943551c393648a1293d55d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$L-$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"28\" height=\"16\" />shape is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/2/a0209a5f3243dcc17c9ae0cce0fde9246f25dc04.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac {1}{4} \\cdot \\frac {2}{9} = \\frac {1}{18}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"93\" height=\"38\" />\nTherefore the probability that the point is inside the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/f/4bfa3d4028c17abddf943551c393648a1293d55d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$L-$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"28\" height=\"16\" />shape is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/4/f54f6836447c0bd0c0c4573a87b92eb39d8ef74a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1-\\frac{1}{18}=\\frac{17}{18} \\implies \\boxed{35}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"186\" height=\"38\" />\n</p><p><b>vladimir.shelomovskii@gmail.com, vvsss</b>\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_4_.28Coordinates.2C_similar_to_Solution_1.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_4_(Coordinates,_similar_to_Solution_1)\">Solution 4 (Coordinates, similar to Solution 1)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 4 (Coordinates, similar to Solution 1)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Consider this diagram:\n</p><p><span typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:Screenshot_2024-01-10_101738.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/4/44/Screenshot_2024-01-10_101738.png/500px-Screenshot_2024-01-10_101738.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"500\" height=\"419\" class=\"mw-file-element\" srcset=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/4/44/Screenshot_2024-01-10_101738.png/750px-Screenshot_2024-01-10_101738.png 1.5x, https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/4/44/Screenshot_2024-01-10_101738.png 2x\" /></a></span>\n</p><p>First, the one of points must be in the uppermost box and the other in the rightmost box. This happens with probability <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/c/1/5c10b0c9f2ece68c811095e787dcdbc0ab28961f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{2}{3}*\\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{2}{9}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"82\" height=\"38\" />.\n</p><p>We need the midpoints of the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> coordinates to be greater than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> but less than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/4/7/e4797950b5e812b65381cbc5fc33ad2db5f57aff.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> \n</p><p>We need the midpoints of the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> coordinates to be greater than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> but less than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/4/7/e4797950b5e812b65381cbc5fc33ad2db5f57aff.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> \n</p><p>Thus, we set up:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/2/b/12b7738ffa5ebeb4e53ee7e693a31ad04cfcf90c.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[1&lt;\\frac{x_1+x_2}{2}&lt;2\\]\" width=\"126\" height=\"35\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/c/e/3cee7423c36f3885523ac85cebcb02acd8436ad8.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[1&lt;\\frac{y_1+y_2}{2}&lt;2\\]\" width=\"123\" height=\"35\" />\n</p><p>Using geometric probability, as shown below, we get that the probability of happening is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/f/9/2f960094315d60883495f9b74148e17487ee9584.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" />. This is for both. So we have probability <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/8/5/b85f2ad31b3127b46f4aba38a9c365f84cc1bfc1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{2}*\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"82\" height=\"38\" />.\n</p><p><span typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:Screenshot_2024-01-10_101905.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/a/a2/Screenshot_2024-01-10_101905.png/500px-Screenshot_2024-01-10_101905.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"500\" height=\"410\" class=\"mw-file-element\" srcset=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/a/a2/Screenshot_2024-01-10_101905.png/750px-Screenshot_2024-01-10_101905.png 1.5x, https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/a/a2/Screenshot_2024-01-10_101905.png 2x\" /></a></span>\n</p><p>Thus, the fraction is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/a/6/3a6f4235d54ac68955f4db04a42fc8d0451c43e7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1-(\\frac{2}{9}*\\frac{1}{4})= 1-\\frac{1}{18}=\\frac{17}{18}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"215\" height=\"38\" />.\n</p><p>The answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/e/09ed4026a5d299a9f91d40b03228d7a4fae52cd7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$17+18=35$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"103\" height=\"15\" />\n</p><p>mathboy282\n</p><p>Minor edit by whatsoever281\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_5\">Solution 5</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 5\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We casework on the squares the two points are in. Unless the two squares are on opposite corners, which happens with probability <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/4/1/041a0b37ca609642fb34c7c855b9d67ed498a560.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{2}{9}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" />. If the points are on opposite corners, the points must be on the upper half of the splitting diagonal for the midpoint to not be in the square. This occurs with probability <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c339afa03e060d959e4942be7522c0c992f4c41c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" />. We take the complement and get final answer <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/d/d/9ddd3751c1d589f791ede707a19a2f63d28cc2ba.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(\\frac{2}{9})(\\frac{3}{4}) + (\\frac{7}{9}) = \\frac{17}{18}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"152\" height=\"38\" />.\n</p><p>CORRECTION: This solution is wrong. We can have one point above the splitting diagonal and one point below the splitting diagonal and still have the midpoint not be in the square.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_The_Power_of_Logic\">Video Solution by The Power of Logic</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by The Power of Logic\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/KngzOXcmM-E\">https://youtu.be/KngzOXcmM-E</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_also\">See also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=9\" title=\"Edit section: See also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AIME_II\" title=\"2023 AIME II\">2023 AIME II</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AIME_II_Problems\" title=\"2023 AIME II Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AIME_II_Answer_Key\" title=\"2023 AIME II Answer Key\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/Forum/resources.php?c=182&amp;cid=45&amp;year=2023\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"2023 AIME II Problems/Problem 5\">Problem 5</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_7\" title=\"2023 AIME II Problems/Problem 7\">Problem 7</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"2023 AIME II Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"2023 AIME II Problems/Problem 2\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"2023 AIME II Problems/Problem 3\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"2023 AIME II Problems/Problem 4\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"2023 AIME II Problems/Problem 5\">5</a> <b>•</b> <a class=\"mw-selflink selflink\">6</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_7\" title=\"2023 AIME II Problems/Problem 7\">7</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_8\" title=\"2023 AIME II Problems/Problem 8\">8</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_9\" title=\"2023 AIME II Problems/Problem 9\">9</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_10\" title=\"2023 AIME II Problems/Problem 10\">10</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_11\" title=\"2023 AIME II Problems/Problem 11\">11</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_12\" title=\"2023 AIME II Problems/Problem 12\">12</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_13\" title=\"2023 AIME II Problems/Problem 13\">13</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_14\" title=\"2023 AIME II Problems/Problem 14\">14</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_15\" title=\"2023 AIME II Problems/Problem 15\">15</a>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=AIME_Problems_and_Solutions\" title=\"AIME Problems and Solutions\"> All AIME Problems and Solutions</a></b>\n</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<div class=\"print\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;width:100%;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center\">\n<div>\n<p><span class=\"mw-default-size\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:AMC_Logo.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/d/d1/AMC_Logo.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"97\" height=\"84\" class=\"mw-file-element\" /></a></span>\n</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex-grow:1;text-align:right\">\n<p>These problems are copyrighted &#169; by the <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://www.maa.org\">Mathematical Association of America</a>. \n</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"clear:both;\"></div>\n</div></div>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261017233624\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.055 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.087 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 516/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 1806/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 128/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 3/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 16923/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%   12.672      1 -total\n 58.39%    7.399      1 Template:AIME_box\n 39.88%    5.053      1 Template:MAA_Notice\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:23919:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261017233624 and revision id 277135. Rendering was triggered because: page-view\n -->\n</div>"}